WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.500
How did Brad Pitt's brutal brawls in&nbsp;
"Fight Club" lead to his cameo in one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.501
of the highest grossing R-rated movies&nbsp;
of all time? Stick around to find out.

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:11.590
Brad Pitt is one of the most&nbsp;
recognizable actors in Hollywood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:16.440
having starred in profitable pictures like&nbsp;
"World War Z," "Mr. and Mrs. Smith," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:20.940
"Ocean's Eleven." But his highest-grossing&nbsp;
film ever doesn't feature him in a leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:27.206
role. That honor actually goes to "Deadpool&nbsp;
2," which made $786 million worldwide.

00:00:27.206 --> 00:00:32.040
It's easy to forget that Pitt was in "Deadpool&nbsp;
2" at all — and if you blinked while watching it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:37.080
you might've missed him. As the Vanisher, he&nbsp;
has the power of total invisibility — but that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:41.290
doesn't stop him from suffering a shocking&nbsp;
fate in a tragic parachuting accident.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:45.420
"Vanisher… maybe the wind&nbsp;
can't blow what it can't see."

00:00:48.208 --> 00:00:49.208
"Really?!"

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:54.000
Yes, this is the only time Pitt actually&nbsp;
appears in the film. He never says a word.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.360
Obviously, the gag is that no one&nbsp;
expected a star of Pitt's caliber&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.740
to join the cast of "Deadpool 2" for such&nbsp;
a miniscule role. Most film productions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:05.220
would be thrilled to have an A-lister&nbsp;
like him on board, but director David&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:09.561
Leitch subverted expectations by&nbsp;
giving him a laughably brief cameo.

00:01:09.561 --> 00:01:15.780
So how did this zany appearance come about? As&nbsp;
Pitt explained to BBC Radio 1 in August 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:20.581
he and Leitch have a friendship that dates all the&nbsp;
way back to the "Fight Club" days. He explained —

00:01:20.581 --> 00:01:25.320
"Dave's an old friend of mine. He was my stunt&nbsp;
double, starting with 'Fight Club' and all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:30.660
way up until about [2004]. And then he went off&nbsp;
and became a really good director, which is rare."

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:34.800
Leitch is best known now as a director&nbsp;
of adrenaline-pumping films like "Bullet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:39.660
Train," "Fast &amp; Furious Presents: Hobbs &amp;&nbsp;
Shaw," and "Atomic Blonde." But to reach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:44.220
his level of success within the action genre,&nbsp;
Leitch first put a lot of time into crafting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:49.860
coordinating, and performing stunts. Aside from&nbsp;
standing in as Tyler Durden in the late '90s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:54.960
Leitch performed stunt work in "Blade" and "The&nbsp;
Matrix Reloaded" — but he still continued to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:59.761
double for Pitt in films like "The Mexican,"&nbsp;
"Ocean's Eleven," and "Mr. &amp; Mrs. Smith."

00:01:59.761 --> 00:02:03.060
After some time, however, their&nbsp;
careers diverged. Leitch pivoted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:07.080
to assisting with stunt coordination&nbsp;
in films like "V for Vendetta," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:11.335
acted as the action coordinator for&nbsp;
"X-Men Origins: Wolverine." Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.335 --> 00:02:15.000
Pitt starred in critically acclaimed films&nbsp;
like "12 Years a Slave," "The Big Short,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:19.434
and "Once Upon a Time in Hollywood" — the&nbsp;
last of which finally got him his Oscar.

00:02:19.434 --> 00:02:24.540
However, Leitch's professional relationship with&nbsp;
Pitt has recently come full circle. During a press&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:28.440
event for Leitch's "Bullet Train," in which&nbsp;
Pitt stars as the bumbling assassin Ladybug,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:31.920
the actor discussed what it was like&nbsp;
to work with Leitch again, saying —

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:36.660
"He trained me for those fights. In a way,&nbsp;
he's helping me develop the character. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:40.920
he's a director with his own voice and&nbsp;
vernacular. I'm serving him. He's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.868
boss. So there was a beautiful&nbsp;
symmetry for two old friends."

00:02:43.868 --> 00:02:48.188
During a conversation with Leitch and Pitt&nbsp;
for Brut America, Leitch happily noted —

00:02:48.188 --> 00:02:52.524
"Our fates took us in different places, and&nbsp;
then they ended up bringing us back together."

00:02:52.524 --> 00:02:56.940
As entertaining as it would be, it's very&nbsp;
unlikely that "Deadpool 3" will be what unites&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:00.840
Leitch and Pitt again for outlandish&nbsp;
battle sequences. Instead of Leitch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:05.400
Shawn Levy will be directing the third Ryan&nbsp;
Reynolds-starring Deadpool film —and unless some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:10.560
kind of multiversal plot device changes things,&nbsp;
Pitt's run as the invisible X-Force member seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:15.120
to be over. It's also unclear when "Deadpool&nbsp;
3" will actually hit the big screen — due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:19.312
to the dual writer and actor strikes within&nbsp;
Hollywood, the release date is up in the air.

00:03:19.312 --> 00:03:22.560
That doesn't mean that the Leitch-Pitt&nbsp;
collaborations are a thing of the past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:26.880
though. "Bullet Train" grossed&nbsp;
over $239 million at the global&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:31.080
box office on a budget of around&nbsp;
$85 million dollars, which is no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:35.576
small feat. It's a testament to the pair's&nbsp;
successful work together on and off camera.

